  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Daily chances for showers and thunderstorms \ninto the middle of the week. There is a risk for strong to \nsevere thunderstorms, as well as a marginal risk for localized \nheavy rainfall leading to flash flooding.\n\nA surface cold front will gradually sag southward across the\nnorthern tier of the region Tuesday afternoon into evening\nbefore stalling around the I-80 or I-78 corridors come \nWednesday. During this time, a few upper level shortwave pulses\nwill rendezvous with the surface cold front, bringing rounds of\nshowers and storms through Wednesday morning. The first round \nof storms Tuesday afternoon and evening will be displaced to our\nsouthwest, but we could still see isolated to scattered \nconvection during the afternoon, or even get the remnants of \nwhatever occurs upstream later in the evening. Any potential MCS\ndevelopment upstream across the Ohio Valley will be difficult \nfor hi res guidance to model, thus leading to a higher-than- \nusual level of uncertainty with the forecast for Tuesday \nafternoon and evening. That said, instability across the region \ndoes look to be limited to the 1000-2000 J/kg range with limited\ndeep layer shear, which will, therefore, limit the severe \nweather potential Tuesday. SPC has expanded the Marginal Risk \n(level 1 out of 5) farther northeast now into the Lehigh Valley \nand central NJ, and the entire area remains in the Marginal Risk\n(level 1 out of 4) for excessive rainfall.\n\nThe latest hi res guidance now suggests a second round of\nshowers and perhaps some convective thunderstorms to arrive\nearly Wednesday morning with the potential for heavy downpours\nduring the Wednesday morning rush hour. Quite a bit of \nuncertainty with this setup still, however, since guidance \nsuggests this round may be a result of a decaying upstream MCS \nfrom the Ohio Valley. This setup remains highly uncertain and \nfully dependent on how the volatile setup across the Ohio Valley\neventually pans out through the overnight hours.\n\nAdditional rounds of upper level shortwaves look to ride along \nthe quasi-stationary boundary which will support rounds of \nconvection continuing at least through Wednesday night, and \npossibly into Thursday. The front looks to push through by late \nThursday, and a much drier and cooler airmass will arrive by \nFriday and Saturday as a result. This will bring us a more \npronounced break in convective potential and more pleasant \nweather conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions will persist into the \nmiddle of the week, but humidity will not be as oppressive as \nrecent days.\n\nA relatively stagnant synoptic pattern will remain in place \nacross our region. Quasi zonal mid and upper level flow will be \nin place through the middle of the week as Bermuda high pressure\npersists offshore. This will keep a very warm and moist airmass\nin place across the region through the period.\n\nHot and humid conditions will continue into the middle of the\nweek, although slightly lower temperatures and dewpoints will\narrive for Wednesday. Dewpoints are anticipated to mix out into\nthe mid to upper 60s again this afternoon. This will give some \nslight reprieve from the excessive humidity we've experienced in\nrecent days, though highs will still be in the upper 80s to mid\n90s. The lower dewpoints will result in maximum heat indices \nmainly in the mid to upper 90s, though could get near 100 \ndegrees in Delmarva and near the coast in New Jersey. Therefore,\nhot and humid, but not to the point where any heat headlines are\nneeded.\n\nBy Friday and Saturday with the front finally having pushed through,\na much drier and more cooler airmass will arrive, which will bring\nmuch more pleasant weather conditions. Dewpoints may drop into the\n50s again for many areas with highs forecast to be in the low to\nmid 80s. Dewpoints look to start rebounding into the 60s again \nby Sunday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...There is potential for minor tidal flooding \nduring the evening high tides this week along the Atlantic \ncoast, Delaware Bay, and tidal Delaware River, especially on \nTuesday and Wednesday.\n\nAs we approach the New Moon on Wednesday, astronomical tides for\nthe evening high tide cycles will increase to around a half foot\nbelow minor flood stage in many areas this week along the \nAtlantic coasts of New Jersey and Delware, Delaware Bay, and \nthe tidal Delaware River. With tide departures running around a \nhalf foot above average, this will raise a threat of minor flood\nstage being reached for these areas.\n\nWe will continue to monitor the tide departures in the coming\ntotal water level forecasts. Coastal Flood Advisories have been\nissued for areas along the Delaware Bay for the Tuesday evening\nhigh tide. Additional Coastal Flood Advisories may be needed \nover the coming days, but at least spotty minor tidal flooding \nshould be anticipated around the times of the evening high tides\nthis week. The highest astronomical tides will occur on \nWednesday, though Tuesday's tides are of similar height. T",
